WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.437 --> 00:00:13.220
BRIAN SCHWARZ: Hi, I'm Brian,
and welcome to our session

00:00:13.220 --> 00:00:14.660
at Google Cloud Next.

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:17.960
This session is going to be
about achieving high resiliency

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:20.630
and availability with
Google Cloud infrastructure.

00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:23.330
I'm joined by my friend and
colleague, Chris Schilling.

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:25.340
We're both product
managers at Google

00:00:25.340 --> 00:00:28.310
and are excited to talk
to you about this session.

00:00:28.310 --> 00:00:31.440
The session today is going
to cover a number of topics.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:33.230
First we're going
to talk about why

00:00:33.230 --> 00:00:36.380
resiliency and high availability
matter in the Cloud,

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:39.530
a little bit about what Google
Cloud provides to customers

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:42.470
as a foundation of the
platform, some of the things

00:00:42.470 --> 00:00:44.180
you as a user of
Google Cloud need

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:46.700
to take into account as you
architect your solutions

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:48.412
and applications
on Google Cloud,

00:00:48.412 --> 00:00:50.870
and then Chris is going to talk
a little bit about business

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:53.810
continuity planning, and it's a
good idea for all applications

00:00:53.810 --> 00:00:55.700
and all systems, and
then we'll give you

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:58.847
some links about how to
find more information.

00:00:58.847 --> 00:01:00.680
With this, I'd like to
turn it over to Chris

00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:02.917
to get into the content.

00:01:02.917 --> 00:01:04.209
CHRIS SCHILLING: Thanks, Brian.

00:01:04.209 --> 00:01:05.667
Now let's talk
about why resiliency

00:01:05.667 --> 00:01:07.690
and high availability
matter to technologists

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:08.607
building in the Cloud.

00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:12.740
Terms like resiliency
can be used

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:15.810
in a lot of different
contexts in IT infrastructure.

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:18.890
We hear the term in the
context of distributed systems,

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:23.863
context of databases, in
the context of networking.

00:01:23.863 --> 00:01:25.530
When we think broadly
about what defines

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:29.460
resilient infrastructure,
there are two key components.

00:01:29.460 --> 00:01:31.170
First off, that
infrastructure needs

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:34.920
to be able to handle failures
without unexpected data loss.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:36.420
If there is a network
partition that

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:38.520
results in the loss of
a node, for example,

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:40.775
a resilient distributed
database with many nodes

00:01:40.775 --> 00:01:42.150
should be able to
recover without

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:44.340
a split-brain situation.

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:46.800
Similarly, if there is a
disk failure in a resilient

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:48.660
enterprise storage
array, the system

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:51.285
should have copies of that data
available elsewhere to mitigate

00:01:51.285 --> 00:01:53.020
against data loss.

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:54.990
So that's the first
component of resiliency.

00:01:54.990 --> 00:01:58.400
Failure should not
mean data loss.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:00.520
The second component is
that resiliency needs

00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:02.830
more than one layer of defense.

00:02:02.830 --> 00:02:05.200
Just as infrastructure
has multiple components,

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:07.810
from software-defined storage
to the relational database,

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:11.720
to network and access security,
to business logic middle tier,

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:13.730
so too does resiliency.

00:02:13.730 --> 00:02:15.730
Resilient infrastructure
will have defense baked

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:18.010
into those layers,
and in certain layers,

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:20.440
multiple lines of defense.

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:22.750
If resiliency is a
fundamentally-defensive concept

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:25.180
focused on your data,
high availability,

00:02:25.180 --> 00:02:28.690
on the other hand, is about
your end user's experience.

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:30.760
Highly-available
applications are still

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:33.130
available to the user
even when something goes

00:02:33.130 --> 00:02:34.763
wrong with the infrastructure.

00:02:34.763 --> 00:02:37.180
That means that your users
shouldn't be adversely affected

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:39.690
when a failure occurs.

00:02:39.690 --> 00:02:43.410
An application can be resilient
without being highly available,

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:46.620
or it could be highly
available without resiliency.

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:47.940
We'd all like to achieve both.

00:02:50.765 --> 00:02:52.140
And that's why we
at Google Cloud

00:02:52.140 --> 00:02:54.350
view these goals as linked.

00:02:54.350 --> 00:02:56.030
If your goal is
high availability

00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:58.310
for a global
consumer application,

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:00.410
you want to ensure that
an individual hardware

00:03:00.410 --> 00:03:02.390
failure in one of
your cloud regions

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:06.230
would not impact your
customer's experience.

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:08.630
That almost always means
thinking about resiliency,

00:03:08.630 --> 00:03:12.700
because data loss can be harmful
to your customers, as well.

00:03:12.700 --> 00:03:14.910
Similarly, if your
goal is resiliency,

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:17.010
you want to make sure that
all your defensive hard

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:19.050
work to protect
against data loss

00:03:19.050 --> 00:03:21.720
and to create layers
of defense will also

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:24.330
have a direct business
impact, ideally

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:26.490
because your
customers never notice

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:29.400
any change to their experience,
even when things go wrong.

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:35.220
So those are linked
goals, both resiliency

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:37.850
and high availability.

00:03:37.850 --> 00:03:39.940
When we break down the
requirements for this,

00:03:39.940 --> 00:03:41.420
I think the first
one on our list

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:43.890
is infrastructure durability.

00:03:43.890 --> 00:03:46.340
It lines up more with the
defensive nature of resiliency

00:03:46.340 --> 00:03:49.610
because here we're talking
about providing hardware SLAs,

00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:52.460
we're talking about making
sure our data centers are safe,

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:54.720
and we're thinking about how
we respond to escalations

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:57.270
within our network.

00:03:57.270 --> 00:03:59.640
A good example of
infrastructure durability

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:02.460
is our public durability service
level objective for Persistent

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:06.980
Disk, our block storage offering
For regional balance PD,

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:13.250
for example, we share that
we had better than 99.9999%

00:04:13.250 --> 00:04:17.320
durability, six nines.

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:19.777
Next up is application
availability.

00:04:19.777 --> 00:04:22.110
That's something that veers
toward the high availability

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:24.610
goal, because we're thinking
about how to keep a database up

00:04:24.610 --> 00:04:26.808
and running through your
transactional applications

00:04:26.808 --> 00:04:29.100
or how to move traffic to
the right node or even region

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:32.290
within a distributed
application.

00:04:32.290 --> 00:04:35.050
A great product level example
is our high availability

00:04:35.050 --> 00:04:38.485
configuration for Cloud SQL,
our managed database offering.

00:04:38.485 --> 00:04:40.450
It lets you set up an
architecture designed

00:04:40.450 --> 00:04:45.580
to limit user impact if the
primary database system fails.

00:04:45.580 --> 00:04:48.400
Last but not least is
business continuity.

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:50.920
We view this as a core
component for both linked goals

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:53.080
because this is all
about planning, checking,

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:55.630
and double checking that you
have an infrastructure safety

00:04:55.630 --> 00:04:56.920
net.

00:04:56.920 --> 00:05:00.155
That means taking backups
to ensure resiliency,

00:05:00.155 --> 00:05:03.160
and it also means testing your
active-active disaster recovery

00:05:03.160 --> 00:05:05.200
architecture to ensure
high availability

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:07.147
for your application.

00:05:07.147 --> 00:05:08.730
One product that we
have in this space

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:11.520
is our multi-region storage,
which stores your backup data

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:14.850
across multiple Google Cloud
regions in a given continent.

00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:17.730
The result is that it's easy
to restore in multiple Google

00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:20.670
Cloud regions on that continent,
depending on what your business

00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:21.900
continuity plan calls for.

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:26.400
What I would take
away from all this

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:28.692
is that Google Cloud delivers
infrastructure durability

00:05:28.692 --> 00:05:31.740
to all our infrastructure
as a service customers.

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:34.140
This is a default setting
for us, not an upcharge

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:35.520
or an option.

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:38.040
If you want to build a
resilient, highly-available

00:05:38.040 --> 00:05:42.880
application, we want to
take care of the first step.

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:46.200
The next two steps, ensuring
application availability

00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:48.840
and creating a business
continuity plan of attack,

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:50.940
those are equally important.

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:53.370
For them, we offer our
customers tools and services

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:57.200
that they can adopt to
meet specific goals.

00:05:57.200 --> 00:06:00.020
We recognize that some tier one
customer-facing transactional

00:06:00.020 --> 00:06:01.550
applications will
have requirements

00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:04.010
above and beyond an
internal sandbox application

00:06:04.010 --> 00:06:09.730
with a few users and no
customer data, for example.

00:06:09.730 --> 00:06:11.940
So let's talk about
durability first.

00:06:11.940 --> 00:06:13.710
It's a great topic
to kick us off

00:06:13.710 --> 00:06:17.575
because Google Cloud
really shines here.

00:06:17.575 --> 00:06:19.515
All our customers
get the benefit

00:06:19.515 --> 00:06:20.890
of infrastructure
durability that

00:06:20.890 --> 00:06:23.950
underpins some of the world's
favorite applications,

00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:25.810
like YouTube.

00:06:25.810 --> 00:06:30.090
And that starts with storage
for all types of applications.

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:32.760
Our block offering,
Persistent Disk,

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:35.920
our first [INAUDIBLE]
file offering, Filestore,

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:39.090
and our object storage
offering, Cloud Storage,

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:41.950
all count some of the
world's largest enterprises

00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:44.500
as customers and users.

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:46.960
Regardless of whether you're
a startup or a Fortune 100

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:50.050
company, you can find the same
public durability information

00:06:50.050 --> 00:06:53.410
under Persistent Disk
offerings on our website.

00:06:53.410 --> 00:06:55.780
All that information
is available publicly.

00:06:55.780 --> 00:06:58.330
We recently published a blog
post to help customers further

00:06:58.330 --> 00:07:01.270
understand how we
approach this durability

00:07:01.270 --> 00:07:05.550
and what we do to keep
your infrastructure safe.

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:07.380
Similarly, because
we're a service provider

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:10.800
for the infrastructure, we have
operational safeguards in place

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:14.620
to make sure you have a
trusted platform to build on.

00:07:14.620 --> 00:07:17.865
That means having encryption
by default, for example,

00:07:17.865 --> 00:07:19.990
so you don't have to worry
about accidental leakage

00:07:19.990 --> 00:07:22.660
of unencrypted data.

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:25.540
It also means having Site
Reliability Engineering,

00:07:25.540 --> 00:07:29.420
or SRE, coverage across a
growing global footprint

00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:32.140
and across all of our services.

00:07:32.140 --> 00:07:34.720
Many of Google's
SREs have contributed

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:36.850
to the open literature
and public practices

00:07:36.850 --> 00:07:39.640
that help make all of us in
product engineering better

00:07:39.640 --> 00:07:41.700
at our operations.

00:07:41.700 --> 00:07:44.250
You get the benefit of all
this knowledge and the benefit

00:07:44.250 --> 00:07:47.310
of having these SREs available
to address service-level issues

00:07:47.310 --> 00:07:49.750
when you build on Google Cloud.

00:07:49.750 --> 00:07:52.110
I'll hand the rest of
the slide over to Brian.

00:07:52.110 --> 00:07:53.900
BRIAN SCHWARZ: Thanks, Chris.

00:07:53.900 --> 00:07:55.940
The other part of
infrastructure durability

00:07:55.940 --> 00:07:59.240
really comes from our global
data centers and our network.

00:07:59.240 --> 00:08:01.910
Our global data centers
are significant.

00:08:01.910 --> 00:08:04.250
We have 27 regions
and over 82 zones

00:08:04.250 --> 00:08:06.860
built out in many
continents in the world,

00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:09.620
and they offer a tremendous
amount of on-demand capacity

00:08:09.620 --> 00:08:11.840
and many layers of physical
security in the data

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:13.130
centers themselves.

00:08:13.130 --> 00:08:16.280
Of course, we've layered on
top of this infrastructure

00:08:16.280 --> 00:08:18.860
sophisticated hardware
monitoring, and all of these

00:08:18.860 --> 00:08:21.980
are used to basically offer
the many managed services that

00:08:21.980 --> 00:08:24.680
are part of the
Google Cloud network.

00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:26.030
And of course, the network--

00:08:26.030 --> 00:08:29.300
I always like to tell people
the Google Cloud network scales

00:08:29.300 --> 00:08:31.550
and has high availability
because Google

00:08:31.550 --> 00:08:34.010
itself has scale and
high availability.

00:08:34.010 --> 00:08:37.490
Almost everybody in the
world has some appreciation

00:08:37.490 --> 00:08:39.570
for the scale of Google's
consumer services.

00:08:39.570 --> 00:08:42.650
You probably use some of
these almost every day.

00:08:42.650 --> 00:08:44.450
These same pieces
of infrastructure

00:08:44.450 --> 00:08:47.120
and the same network is used
to provide the capabilities

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:49.650
that we offer in the
Google Cloud platform.

00:08:49.650 --> 00:08:51.740
And of course, we have
significant investments

00:08:51.740 --> 00:08:55.507
in undersea cabling, edge
PoPs, or Points Of Presence,

00:08:55.507 --> 00:08:57.590
where we essentially are
peering into the internet

00:08:57.590 --> 00:08:59.570
and the network,
and of course, we

00:08:59.570 --> 00:09:01.250
have thousands of
edge caches to make

00:09:01.250 --> 00:09:03.510
the performance of Google
services and, of course,

00:09:03.510 --> 00:09:05.870
the performance of many
Google Cloud services

00:09:05.870 --> 00:09:08.460
incredibly performant
and available.

00:09:08.460 --> 00:09:10.490
The other thing I'd like
to use is an example

00:09:10.490 --> 00:09:13.273
is our networking prowess and
the software-defined networking

00:09:13.273 --> 00:09:15.440
we have in Google Cloud
makes it really easy for you

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:18.470
to build on top of this
global infrastructure.

00:09:18.470 --> 00:09:21.230
A specific example of
that is the global VPCs,

00:09:21.230 --> 00:09:23.300
or Virtual Private Clouds,
that you can build.

00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:26.060
They are network constructs
that are very easy to set up

00:09:26.060 --> 00:09:28.263
and essentially span
Google Cloud regions.

00:09:28.263 --> 00:09:30.680
So you can create one virtual
network that spans a region.

00:09:30.680 --> 00:09:33.590
It makes it very easy for you to
build global applications that

00:09:33.590 --> 00:09:35.600
take advantage of
this infrastructure

00:09:35.600 --> 00:09:39.050
that we've built.

00:09:39.050 --> 00:09:40.700
Now let's talk about
some of the things

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:42.200
that it's important
for you to think

00:09:42.200 --> 00:09:44.570
about architecting as
you build applications

00:09:44.570 --> 00:09:46.400
and services on Google Cloud.

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:48.770
And it's important to
think about applications

00:09:48.770 --> 00:09:50.870
in terms of different
types of applications.

00:09:50.870 --> 00:09:52.940
I often like to think
about, particularly

00:09:52.940 --> 00:09:55.490
in a short session like this,
different types of applications

00:09:55.490 --> 00:09:57.800
that would be like web servers
and application servers,

00:09:57.800 --> 00:10:00.980
often being stateless apps and
a second set of applications

00:10:00.980 --> 00:10:03.860
being databases and very
durable apps, places

00:10:03.860 --> 00:10:05.150
where all of your data lives.

00:10:05.150 --> 00:10:07.525
And we're going to spend a
little time to talk about each

00:10:07.525 --> 00:10:08.653
of those individually.

00:10:11.760 --> 00:10:14.718
So if we look at the left
part of the slide here,

00:10:14.718 --> 00:10:17.010
the first thing I want to
talk about is load balancing.

00:10:17.010 --> 00:10:20.720
So the first two, load balancing
and the GCE, or Google Compute

00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:22.942
Engine, auto healing
are really things

00:10:22.942 --> 00:10:25.400
to first think about when you're
thinking about application

00:10:25.400 --> 00:10:28.190
servers and web servers, that
first category of applications

00:10:28.190 --> 00:10:29.700
I mentioned.

00:10:29.700 --> 00:10:32.880
Load balancers essentially
redirect traffic away

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:35.760
from unresponsive servers,
unresponsive web servers

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:36.990
or application server.

00:10:36.990 --> 00:10:38.130
They may be too busy.

00:10:38.130 --> 00:10:39.552
It might be a software defect.

00:10:39.552 --> 00:10:41.010
There could be a
lot of reasons why

00:10:41.010 --> 00:10:42.660
a node becomes unresponsive.

00:10:42.660 --> 00:10:45.420
Load balancing essentially
will redirect traffic away

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:47.490
from these unresponsive
nodes so your customers

00:10:47.490 --> 00:10:50.727
and users of the applications
get a great experience.

00:10:50.727 --> 00:10:53.310
There are different options on
how you deploy a load balancer.

00:10:53.310 --> 00:10:56.700
You can deploy it to
essentially give capabilities

00:10:56.700 --> 00:11:01.080
to span zones and regions for
more local load balancing.

00:11:01.080 --> 00:11:02.820
There's also options
to essentially do

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:03.900
global load balancing.

00:11:03.900 --> 00:11:05.490
So all of your
consumers and users

00:11:05.490 --> 00:11:07.830
can come into one single
point, and the application

00:11:07.830 --> 00:11:10.290
may actually be spread
across regions, so even

00:11:10.290 --> 00:11:13.890
full region outages will be
hidden from your customers.

00:11:13.890 --> 00:11:18.320
They'll be redirected to
surviving, healthy nodes.

00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:20.060
The second layer of
defense-- as Chris

00:11:20.060 --> 00:11:22.520
mentioned, it's good to have
multiple layers of defense

00:11:22.520 --> 00:11:24.080
for applications
and web servers-- is

00:11:24.080 --> 00:11:26.060
to think about auto healing.

00:11:26.060 --> 00:11:27.830
So in Google
Compute Engine, this

00:11:27.830 --> 00:11:30.890
is our main offering that
essentially offers VMs.

00:11:30.890 --> 00:11:33.500
Many, many applications
run in these VMs,

00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:35.930
and there's a construct
that we've developed

00:11:35.930 --> 00:11:37.850
called managed instance groups.

00:11:37.850 --> 00:11:41.210
It's essentially a set of
VMs that form an application

00:11:41.210 --> 00:11:42.860
tier in their environment.

00:11:42.860 --> 00:11:45.290
They can essentially provide
a second layer of protection

00:11:45.290 --> 00:11:47.090
and availability,
and, essentially,

00:11:47.090 --> 00:11:50.180
they will automatically
restart unresponsive nodes.

00:11:50.180 --> 00:11:52.310
So a node may be too busy.

00:11:52.310 --> 00:11:54.170
It may get somehow
locked in some state

00:11:54.170 --> 00:11:55.850
that it can't recover from.

00:11:55.850 --> 00:11:58.680
The load balancer will
shift traffic away from it,

00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:00.890
and then the GCE auto
healing will come in

00:12:00.890 --> 00:12:02.910
to essentially
restart those nodes.

00:12:02.910 --> 00:12:06.685
So again, further
rehealing the system.

00:12:06.685 --> 00:12:08.060
An important thing
to think about

00:12:08.060 --> 00:12:09.935
as you start to build
these layers of defense

00:12:09.935 --> 00:12:11.180
is how they work together.

00:12:11.180 --> 00:12:13.070
And an example is
you essentially

00:12:13.070 --> 00:12:16.550
want the load balancer
to redirect traffic away

00:12:16.550 --> 00:12:19.430
from an unresponsive node
before it gets restarted.

00:12:19.430 --> 00:12:22.580
So as a perfect example,
you want the health checks

00:12:22.580 --> 00:12:24.590
that are in your
load balancer to be

00:12:24.590 --> 00:12:26.810
more frequent and aggressive,
and reshift traffic

00:12:26.810 --> 00:12:29.240
away from unresponsive
nodes, and then

00:12:29.240 --> 00:12:33.200
the managed instance group auto
healing feature to kick in also

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:36.050
has health checks to work
slightly slower to actually go

00:12:36.050 --> 00:12:37.640
in and restart nodes.

00:12:37.640 --> 00:12:39.500
That way the system
is, in some senses,

00:12:39.500 --> 00:12:42.178
self-healing or auto-healing.

00:12:42.178 --> 00:12:43.720
The second thing I
want to talk about

00:12:43.720 --> 00:12:45.440
is more on the
stateful application

00:12:45.440 --> 00:12:48.580
tier, which is why I've talked
about Cloud SQL and Spanner.

00:12:48.580 --> 00:12:51.070
So Chris mentioned
Cloud SQL earlier.

00:12:51.070 --> 00:12:53.830
You could of course run
a database inside of one

00:12:53.830 --> 00:12:54.670
of our VMs.

00:12:54.670 --> 00:12:56.200
Many customers
choose to do that.

00:12:56.200 --> 00:12:58.510
But it's also great to
think about managed database

00:12:58.510 --> 00:13:00.190
offerings to reduce
some of the toil

00:13:00.190 --> 00:13:02.360
about setting up and
running a database server.

00:13:02.360 --> 00:13:04.370
And that's really where
Cloud SQL comes in.

00:13:04.370 --> 00:13:06.490
It's a managed database
service we have.

00:13:06.490 --> 00:13:10.390
It supports a lot of common
databases, MySQL, PostgreSQL,

00:13:10.390 --> 00:13:14.290
SQL Server, and it has a number
of interesting capabilities

00:13:14.290 --> 00:13:16.840
that really add value in
the forms of durability

00:13:16.840 --> 00:13:19.060
and resilience and
high availability.

00:13:19.060 --> 00:13:22.150
One is using
snapshot-based backups.

00:13:22.150 --> 00:13:24.650
You can automate it, so
it takes regular backups.

00:13:24.650 --> 00:13:27.640
Let's say every four hours you
have good protection points.

00:13:27.640 --> 00:13:29.800
But you can also use it
in on-demand snapshots

00:13:29.800 --> 00:13:31.258
if you're going to
do a maintenance

00:13:31.258 --> 00:13:33.730
task on your database,
add tables, change

00:13:33.730 --> 00:13:34.750
the scheme, et cetera.

00:13:34.750 --> 00:13:39.540
You can create a snapshot to
create an easy recovery point.

00:13:39.540 --> 00:13:40.980
The managed database
service also

00:13:40.980 --> 00:13:44.310
has a built in
capability to offer you

00:13:44.310 --> 00:13:46.085
point-in-time recovery
for your databases.

00:13:46.085 --> 00:13:48.210
So you have snapshots, and
then you can essentially

00:13:48.210 --> 00:13:50.760
move the database to a
particular point in time

00:13:50.760 --> 00:13:52.722
based on the logs that
are in the database.

00:13:52.722 --> 00:13:54.930
So it's a combination of
the managed database service

00:13:54.930 --> 00:13:56.730
working in conjunction
with snapshots

00:13:56.730 --> 00:13:58.530
to offer you a
capability to really

00:13:58.530 --> 00:14:00.330
be specific at exactly
the point where

00:14:00.330 --> 00:14:02.400
the database is
consistent and you want

00:14:02.400 --> 00:14:05.130
to restart the application.

00:14:05.130 --> 00:14:07.890
It's also great to think
about the high availability

00:14:07.890 --> 00:14:10.890
capabilities in the
Cloud SQL offering,

00:14:10.890 --> 00:14:13.330
because it offers
failover replicas.

00:14:13.330 --> 00:14:15.870
These are essentially shadow
databases that can be set up.

00:14:15.870 --> 00:14:19.260
There are options inside the
service that can essentially

00:14:19.260 --> 00:14:22.840
run in multiple zones to
protect you from a zone outage.

00:14:22.840 --> 00:14:25.232
These zonal things can use
the regional PD offering,

00:14:25.232 --> 00:14:27.690
the regional Persistent Disk
offering that Chris mentioned,

00:14:27.690 --> 00:14:30.600
so we're synchronously
replicating the data

00:14:30.600 --> 00:14:33.100
for the database
underneath the covers.

00:14:33.100 --> 00:14:36.460
And then you essentially
have a Cloud SQL instance

00:14:36.460 --> 00:14:39.780
that's ready to go in the
event of a zonal outage.

00:14:39.780 --> 00:14:42.150
There's also a
capability in Cloud SQL

00:14:42.150 --> 00:14:46.410
to essentially do cross
region protection.

00:14:46.410 --> 00:14:48.930
This is, of course, an
asynchronous capability,

00:14:48.930 --> 00:14:51.840
but it can also protect you
against full regional outages.

00:14:51.840 --> 00:14:54.300
So, again, there's a lot
of power and flexibility

00:14:54.300 --> 00:14:56.220
in terms of the
offering that Cloud SQL

00:14:56.220 --> 00:14:57.960
has in terms of
building in resilience

00:14:57.960 --> 00:14:59.700
and high availability.

00:14:59.700 --> 00:15:01.710
The last thing I wanted
to think about or talk

00:15:01.710 --> 00:15:03.330
to you about in
the database tier

00:15:03.330 --> 00:15:06.090
is our Spanner
offering, Cloud Spanner.

00:15:06.090 --> 00:15:08.940
This is a really
powerful capability.

00:15:08.940 --> 00:15:10.500
It's highly differentiated.

00:15:10.500 --> 00:15:12.480
It really comes from
technology that Google

00:15:12.480 --> 00:15:15.330
built to scale to the
billions of users we have

00:15:15.330 --> 00:15:17.400
on our consumer applications.

00:15:17.400 --> 00:15:19.330
It's a fully-managed
database service,

00:15:19.330 --> 00:15:20.970
so it's very easy to use.

00:15:20.970 --> 00:15:24.780
But it's basically a single
cross regional database.

00:15:24.780 --> 00:15:27.558
It's active-active and
read/write in many regions

00:15:27.558 --> 00:15:30.100
all at the same time, and that's
really what makes it unique.

00:15:30.100 --> 00:15:32.970
So it preserves the
value of standard SQL,

00:15:32.970 --> 00:15:35.610
because that's such a common
application programming

00:15:35.610 --> 00:15:39.735
language, but it provides you
essentially, in some senses,

00:15:39.735 --> 00:15:41.760
you can think about a
cross-regional database

00:15:41.760 --> 00:15:44.440
as a continent-sized database
for you to operate on.

00:15:44.440 --> 00:15:46.830
So it really allows you
to simplify how you think

00:15:46.830 --> 00:15:48.210
about building these systems.

00:15:48.210 --> 00:15:50.760
And it comes with a
five-nine availability SLA,

00:15:50.760 --> 00:15:53.340
so it's really a
powerful capability

00:15:53.340 --> 00:15:55.710
to have in the arsenal as
you think about how you build

00:15:55.710 --> 00:15:58.140
applications on Google Cloud.

00:15:58.140 --> 00:16:02.315
Now I'd like to turn it over
to Chris for the next part.

00:16:02.315 --> 00:16:04.300
CHRIS SCHILLING: Much
appreciate it, Brian.

00:16:04.300 --> 00:16:06.580
So let's switch back
to business continuity,

00:16:06.580 --> 00:16:09.340
the insurance policy, the safety
net that we all know we need.

00:16:11.960 --> 00:16:14.320
Business continuity is
a pretty expansive term.

00:16:14.320 --> 00:16:16.820
It can mean a lot of things
from how a cloud architect plans

00:16:16.820 --> 00:16:19.340
for disaster,
production [INAUDIBLE],,

00:16:19.340 --> 00:16:23.182
to how a storage
administrator tests backups.

00:16:23.182 --> 00:16:25.390
You'll noticed I mentioned
planning and testing right

00:16:25.390 --> 00:16:28.720
off the bat, and we have a
number of features and services

00:16:28.720 --> 00:16:31.420
built for business
continuity purposes, features

00:16:31.420 --> 00:16:33.565
like being able to export
a copy of your database

00:16:33.565 --> 00:16:35.690
to our object storage for
long-term [? archival, ?]

00:16:35.690 --> 00:16:36.730
for example.

00:16:36.730 --> 00:16:39.880
But business continuity
is about being absolutely

00:16:39.880 --> 00:16:42.580
certain that the data
will be available

00:16:42.580 --> 00:16:44.620
when the auditor calls.

00:16:44.620 --> 00:16:47.140
That's why the human element
of planning and testing

00:16:47.140 --> 00:16:48.996
is so important here.

00:16:48.996 --> 00:16:51.745
As the saying goes, an
amateur takes a backup.

00:16:51.745 --> 00:16:53.620
A professional tests
that it can be restored.

00:16:56.870 --> 00:16:58.990
So when we dial into
a couple of services,

00:16:58.990 --> 00:17:01.960
two of interesting ones
that I find differentiated

00:17:01.960 --> 00:17:06.119
are our multi-region storage
and our orchestrated backup.

00:17:06.119 --> 00:17:09.207
They play different roles in
our business continuity package.

00:17:09.207 --> 00:17:11.040
Multi-regional storage
can mean keeping data

00:17:11.040 --> 00:17:12.450
in our object storage.

00:17:12.450 --> 00:17:13.890
We'll keep two
copies of the data

00:17:13.890 --> 00:17:15.810
in two different
regions on a continent,

00:17:15.810 --> 00:17:18.740
available in case of a
regional failure or disaster.

00:17:18.740 --> 00:17:20.740
This also underpins some
of our other offerings.

00:17:20.740 --> 00:17:22.930
It makes it possible, for
example, for our customers

00:17:22.930 --> 00:17:24.670
to restore snapshots
stored in North

00:17:24.670 --> 00:17:28.030
America to different
North American regions

00:17:28.030 --> 00:17:30.010
without having to create
a handful of copies

00:17:30.010 --> 00:17:33.280
across the continent and wait
for them to be replicated one

00:17:33.280 --> 00:17:35.620
after another.

00:17:35.620 --> 00:17:37.750
And on the orchestrated
backup side,

00:17:37.750 --> 00:17:41.800
we recently acquired Actifio,
a proven backup vendor

00:17:41.800 --> 00:17:45.880
that sells Actifio GO, a backup
as a service offering built

00:17:45.880 --> 00:17:48.090
on Google Cloud.

00:17:48.090 --> 00:17:51.690
Actifio GO can protect
Google Compute Engine VMs

00:17:51.690 --> 00:17:53.790
if you're building in the Cloud.

00:17:53.790 --> 00:17:56.820
Or if you're looking at
shifting relational databases

00:17:56.820 --> 00:18:00.810
into Google VMs and you
need application-consistent,

00:18:00.810 --> 00:18:04.740
agent-based backups similar
to what you have on-prem,

00:18:04.740 --> 00:18:07.955
Actifio GO can deliver the
same functionality to you,

00:18:07.955 --> 00:18:10.080
allowing you to backup your
newly-migrated database

00:18:10.080 --> 00:18:13.718
workloads as frequently
as every 15 minutes.

00:18:13.718 --> 00:18:16.260
Best of all, that data can be
stored in multiple Google Cloud

00:18:16.260 --> 00:18:19.590
object storage buckets in
different regions and classes,

00:18:19.590 --> 00:18:22.470
and it can be restored directly
from object storage, which

00:18:22.470 --> 00:18:25.230
means lower long-term
storage costs for customers

00:18:25.230 --> 00:18:27.233
with severe data
retention requirements

00:18:27.233 --> 00:18:29.400
and easier discussions with
your internal compliance

00:18:29.400 --> 00:18:31.930
counterparts when you test
that a backup is available,

00:18:31.930 --> 00:18:35.760
for example, without the need
to complicate restores or move

00:18:35.760 --> 00:18:39.353
data around to different
block storage options.

00:18:39.353 --> 00:18:40.770
These are just two
of our features

00:18:40.770 --> 00:18:42.978
that we've created to make
business continuity easier

00:18:42.978 --> 00:18:46.380
for you to plan and to test
so that you and your team

00:18:46.380 --> 00:18:48.900
can achieve those
goals around resiliency

00:18:48.900 --> 00:18:50.453
and high adaptability.

00:18:50.453 --> 00:18:51.870
If you'd like to
learn more, we've

00:18:51.870 --> 00:18:55.140
included a couple of links
to some interesting content

00:18:55.140 --> 00:18:59.072
on disaster recovery, high
availability, and resilience.

00:18:59.072 --> 00:19:00.890
In the bottom right,
there's a link

00:19:00.890 --> 00:19:06.290
to achieving resiliency and
high availability in Cloud SQL.

00:19:06.290 --> 00:19:08.155
It's a video from
last year's Next,

00:19:08.155 --> 00:19:09.530
and it covers a
lot of the topics

00:19:09.530 --> 00:19:12.260
we've discussed here as they
apply to that managed database

00:19:12.260 --> 00:19:14.210
service.

00:19:14.210 --> 00:19:18.303
Just above that, we have a
DR planning guide for GCE.

00:19:18.303 --> 00:19:20.720
Those of you who are running
stateful workloads and Google

00:19:20.720 --> 00:19:23.900
Cloud VMs, we think you'll
find that useful to help you

00:19:23.900 --> 00:19:27.320
plan for your DR requirements.

00:19:27.320 --> 00:19:30.230
Over on the left, we have
a few sessions of Next

00:19:30.230 --> 00:19:32.250
that we think you'll
find relevant.

00:19:32.250 --> 00:19:35.930
We've included their titles
and the shorthand name

00:19:35.930 --> 00:19:39.650
with numbers so you can
find those in the catalog.

00:19:39.650 --> 00:19:42.050
I want to thank Brian for
co-presenting with me,

00:19:42.050 --> 00:19:45.850
and I want to thank all of
you for attending our session.

